00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.350
so let's hear what he has to say

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.087
about how to structure problems.

00:03:12.087 --> 00:03:15.617
"Feel free to be MECE, to
structure your thinking

00:03:15.617 --> 00:03:19.207
"when solving business problems
or anything for that matter.

00:03:19.207 --> 00:03:22.890
"You must be complete while
avoiding confusion and overlap."

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.750
A good structure for every
problem that you want to solve

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:29.460
at McKinsey is for sure
something that is very important.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.710
MECE is a principle that is
frequently used at McKinsey.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:34.820
Though I will claim that
many other consultancies

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.000
use it as way.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.410
MECE stands for mutually exclusive

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:41.300
and collectively
exhaustive, and it describes

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.830
the way how you should structure problems.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.560
Specifically, it gives you
a rule how you should divide

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.290
a problem into smaller components.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.770
And by following the MECE
principle, the divided components

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:56.010
should on the one hand
be mutually exclusive.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.700
This means that the
components should not overlap.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.910
Every single individual
component should stand for itself

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.110
and not overlap with other components.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.970
And second, it should be
collectively exhaustive.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:10.660
So this means that, taking
all the components together

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.580
should again form the
fully completed problem.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.530
I will insert your picture of a circle

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:18.760
that is divided in a
MECE way from Wikipedia,

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.360
and I hope that this helps you understand

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:21.970
on a more intuitive level

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.670
what a MECE separation of a problem is.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:28.810
In the book, he also makes
references to the famous

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.207
and dreaded up-or-out principle.

00:04:31.207 --> 00:04:33.017
"Associates who perform poorly,

00:04:33.017 --> 00:04:35.137
"didn't last very long at the firm,

00:04:35.137 --> 00:04:37.247
"after one bad engagement,

00:04:37.247 --> 00:04:40.270
"no EM or ED would want them on the team."

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.810
For the uninitiated of you,

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:44.730
what does up-or-out actually mean?

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:47.570
Up-or-out is a principle
that is applied by McKinsey

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:50.810
but also other consulting
firms, which basically means

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.280
that either you get designated
over time to the next level,

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.930
or if you do not make the promotion,

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.680
if you do not make the designation,

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.170
then at some point you will
be asked to leave the firm.

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:02.720
So, it's either you go up or you go out.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.910
And how exactly this
works probably is material

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:07.630
for a whole other video, but
what I can already tell you

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:10.250
is that it is much less cruel and horrible

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:11.690
than what it may be sounds like,

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:13.380
and especially in the lower levels,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:16.580
if you're business analyst,
associate, senior associate.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.650
These things are true, but
there's only a very small portion

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:22.210
of associates is actually
affected by that.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.470
So, there's definitely
no need to be afraid

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.640
that for every little error that you make,

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.400
at some point you will be just
asked to leave the company,

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.090
this is not how it works.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:32.890
And what I especially disagree with

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:34.750
is that the author claims that basically

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:37.410
after only one bad engagement,

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.270
after only one problematic project,

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.890
where you for whatever
reason weren't really able

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.070
to perform in the way that you hope to,

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.480
you would already be on
your way out of the firm,

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:48.820
this is just not true.

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:49.860
From what I have learned,

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.950
also talking to many other associates,

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:54.410
almost every single associate will be able

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:57.650
to tell you one story or
two about this one project

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:00.480
that really went badly,
about this one situation

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.450
where they really made
this crazy, insane mistake.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.160
These things happen, these
things happen to everybody,

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.340
and trust me that everybody

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.270
would get another chance at the firm.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.950
People would try to help you,
will try to work with you.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:14.130
And if there are any specific
things that you struggle with,

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.367
people will try to help you,

00:06:15.367 --> 00:06:17.930
they will really work
hard together with you

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:21.220
to really enable you to
be successful in the firm

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.240
and master this cues
that you need to master

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.550
in order to become a good
and effective consultant.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.550
So, claiming that after one bad project,

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:31.070
your career at McKinsey is
over, is just blatantly wrong,

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:32.670
this is not how it is.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.430
And now way further elaborating

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:36.770
how McKinsey solves problems,

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.857
he talks about the so
called initial hypothesis.

00:06:39.857 --> 00:06:42.207
"Solve the problem at the first meeting

00:06:42.207 --> 00:06:44.077
"The initial hypothesis,

00:06:44.077 --> 00:06:45.587
"solving a complex problem,

00:06:45.587 --> 00:06:47.557
"is like embarking on a long journey.

00:06:47.557 --> 00:06:50.650
"The initial hypothesis is
your problem solving map."

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:52.060
Yes, the book is right here.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:54.680
What is referred to as
an initial hypothesis,

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.440
is often also called the
day one answer at McKinsey,

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.030
the day one answer.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:03.679
So, if you are a project lead
or a partner at McKinsey,

00:07:03.679 --> 00:07:07.010
and you start on a project,
often you're asked to develop

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:11.250
an hypothesis, what the answer
to the problem should be

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.580
that you're solving,
on the very first day.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.110
So very often, in the
very first team meeting,

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.550
and the very first problem solving session

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.530
that the team has, the
team tries to come up

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:22.990
with a day one answer.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:26.480
So with their best understanding,
their best hypothesis

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.210
based on all the information
and facts they know on day one

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:32.790
is what the answer to a
problem should look like.

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:35.400
And the reason why this is
done is that this gives now

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.200
the whole team a direction.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:40.270
So, based on the day one
answer, and all the team starts

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:43.400
to break it down into
different hypotheses.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.560
So, what do you need to believe?

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.120
What do you need to know
in order to understand

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.960
whether this hypothesis,
this day one answer

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:51.793
is actually true?

00:07:51.793 --> 00:07:53.660
And then the team starts to go out,

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:56.260
interview people in the
organization, collect data,

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:59.320
conduct analysis, and to find out

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.770
whether the day one hypothesis
is actually true or not.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.880
And very frequently in the
process after a few days

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:08.370
or maybe also a couple of
weeks, you find out that,

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.270
well no, the day one
answer is not correct.

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:14.530
But actually one of the
hypothesis that the day one answer

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:17.110
was based on was found to be false,

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:18.260
because you did some interviews,

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:19.550
you conducted analysis,

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:21.290
and this is what the reside of that is.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:25.160
So, now you can revise the
day one answer that you have

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.470
based on the new findings, and
then use this revised version

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.000
to conduct further
tests, further analysis,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.080
and then we iterate on this answer

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.440
until you get to the final answer

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:36.740
that everybody believes in.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:38.390
And there are many different reasons

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:41.240
why this hypothesis
driven top down approach

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.760
to problem solving is
actually advantageous

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:46.000
compared to other problem
solving solutions.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.680
And if you're interested to learn more,

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.360
I have created a whole course

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.380
on creating slide presentations in the way

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.630
the top management
consulting firms do that.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:57.410
And this course, I have a whole section

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.470
on structuring presentations,
creating storylines,

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:03.400
and also solving problems
in an hypothesis driven way.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:06.110
So, if you're interested to
learn more about this course,

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:09.000
I included a link to it below
individually description.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.790
The are over 10,000 students
already taking the course,

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:13.780
have a look to see
whether this is something

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:15.140
that might interest you as well.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:18.110
He continues to argue that
why there are similarities

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:21.500
between business problems,
the clients are all different,

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:24.570
and this makes it necessary
for McKinsey consultants

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:26.850
structure completely different solutions

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.287
for every single client.

00:09:28.287 --> 00:09:29.687
"Every client is unique,

00:09:29.687 --> 00:09:31.477
"but that there are many similarities

00:09:31.477 --> 00:09:32.947
"between business problems,

00:09:32.947 --> 00:09:36.927
"does not mean that similar
problems have similar solutions.

00:09:36.927 --> 00:09:40.241
"You have to validate your
initial hypothesis or your gut

00:09:40.241 --> 00:09:42.757
"with fact based analysis.

00:09:42.757 --> 00:09:45.017
"This will put you in
a much better position

00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:46.630
"to get your ideas accepted."

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:49.510
I believe that this section
of the book tries to address

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:53.810
a frequently phrased criticism
of consulting in general,

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:55.860
and maybe also McKinsey in specific,

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.020
which is that consulting firms develop

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:00.160
a solution once for one client,

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.670
and then they take the solution

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:03.280
and said the very same solution

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:05.410
to a whole bunch of other clients.

00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:09.100
And I would argue that this
is true for some aspects,

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:11.540
but wrong for most of the other aspects.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:12.870
What do I mean by that?

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:14.750
It is true that consulting firms

00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:17.910
try to come up with
methodologies and tools

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.390
that they can use not only at one client,

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:22.430
but then at other clients as well.

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:24.550
And by doing that, it really enables them

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:27.740
to validate their concept
to then also refine

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:29.760
these tools based on the learnings

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.010
that they had using them at
other client's situations,

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:35.670
and coming up with a solution
that is really helpful

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:37.790
and really adding value to the client.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:39.000
So yes, this is true,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.110
this is what consulting firms are doing.

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:43.460
And one example could
be a certain framework

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:46.160
of how the consulting firm analyzes

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:48.390
the digitalization strategy of a company.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:50.530
So potentially, they could
come up with a framework

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:53.370
of saying that there are five key areas

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.830
that they would want to
look at when they assess

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:58.260
the digitalization
strategy of the company,

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:01.160
and then each area probably
consists of other elements

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:02.520
that they would want to look at.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.920
And by following this
approach, they would be able

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:08.960
to come to a reliable and
repeatable reside of an assessment

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.290
of how good, how valid and how suitable

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:14.480
the digitalization strategy
is, for a specific company.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.960
So, yes, why they would
reuse this framework

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.810
for several other clients,
what they will not do,

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:21.780
is just to copy paste the solution

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.200
that they found for one client.

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.050
So, it's absolutely true and crucial

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:27.500
that every single situation is different.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.310
Even if you look at companies
in the very same industry,

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:33.340
operating in the very same
field, these companies

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:35.840
are often structured in
a very different way.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:37.960
They operate in a different
way, there are often

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:41.300
some fine and important nuances
in the product portfolio,

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:44.430
and you need to take all
of this into consideration

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:47.730
to come to a solution, so
you just cannot copy paste

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.240
the solution that you just
developed at another client,

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.560
and I've really never seen this happen

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:54.080
during my time at McKinsey.

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:57.980
To the contrary, there is a
very strong spirit in the firm

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:01.870
trying to challenge each member
of the team to really get

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:05.130
to the best possible
solution for our client,

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:08.040
to really seek out all
avenues, all resources

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:10.660
that the firm has available
to get to the solution

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:12.840
that solved the problem for the client

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:14.840
in the very best way possible.

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:17.430
One thing many people
are afraid of especially

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:20.130
when they think of the
MBB consulting firms,

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:23.550
so, McKinsey BCG or
Bain, are the work hours.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:26.907
And in this regard he
tries to give one advice.

00:12:26.907 --> 00:12:29.677
"Make one day a week off limits.

00:12:29.677 --> 00:12:32.987
"Pick a day, most people
take Saturday or Sunday,

00:12:32.987 --> 00:12:36.117
"and tell your boss and
yourself that you never work

00:12:36.117 --> 00:12:39.207
"on the day unless it's
an absolute emergency.

00:12:39.207 --> 00:12:41.577
"Most bosses, at least in my experience,

00:12:41.577 --> 00:12:43.700
"will respect that most of the time."

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:46.570
So, this is where strongly
disagree with the book,

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:49.680
here the author makes it
sound like that on Saturdays.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:52.960
and Sundays, it is a regular
practice that has an associate

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:56.060
or a consultant at McKinsey,
you would need to work.

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:58.590
From my experience, this
is absolutely wrong.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:00.940
While it is no secret
that the work life balance

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:03.080
at McKinsey and also
other consulting firms

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:04.590
is not necessarily great,

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:07.040
and why there are also
differences country by country,

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:09.500
I was part of the German
office at McKinsey,

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.810
and the German office is
considered one of the worst offices

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:14.780
worldwide regarding the working hours.

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:16.850
I guess Germans just have the reputation

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:19.500
of working long hours, and
having worked in Germany,

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:21.910
I can tell you that in
my time at McKinsey,

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:24.210
there were really only
a handful of weekends

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:25.480
where I needed to work,

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:27.790
because there was really
a hard business reason.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:29.720
And actually I found
the contrary to be true,

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:31.520
in the firm there's a strong culture

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.130
to keep the weekends free.

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:36.770
I have seen many project
leads who really tried

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:40.050
to make an effort that whenever
they sense that an associate

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:42.610
fields like, you know, needs
to work on the weekend,

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:44.400
to help him reprioritize his work,

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:46.730
just sit together with
them to make it possible

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:48.470
to just cancel out all the to dos,

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:50.320
to help him to get the weekend free

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:51.880
and not making work necessary.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:54.170
So, if there is just a
situation in your project

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:57.500
to just makes it absolutely
necessary to work on a weekend,

00:13:57.500 --> 00:13:59.720
then yes, you will be asked
to work on the weekend

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.070
and it will be expected
from you that you will make

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.120
the time to do that.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:04.920
But this is, at least from my experience

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.110
having been part of
McKinsey for six years,

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:09.480
this is a clear exception
and not the norm.

